Hi Jim, thanks so much for your reply.

I see your point about the random selection of sites.

This study
was intended to have two complete "seasons" (separated by more than a decade) with all sites surveyed in both seasons (far more than 415 sites) and I originally modeled data from both seasons and all sites.  After some critique, I
became concerned that assumptions of temporal closure were violated as
some within-site replicates occurred over multiple years within each "season".  To remedy this,
 I've had to considerably reduce the number of sites in both seasons (to ~250 in each, with 95 sites occurring in both).  Thus, the selection of the 415 (or 95) sites are dictated by data "issues" rather than randomization.  It's far from ideal, but (eyeballing it) the remaining 415 sites do have a generally random spatial distribution.

Giancarlo

